# Repository Guidelines
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
## Project Structure & Module Organization
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
`app/` contains the Flask application factory, extensions, CLI commands, models, and blueprint packages (`auth`, `main`, `support`, `aid`, `admin`). Shared templates live in `templates/`, with aid-specific pages in `templates/aid/`. Static assets are in `static/`. Database migrations live in `migrations/`. Tests are in `tests/` and generally mirror the features or endpoints they cover. Utility scripts live in `scripts/`. Local runtime data appears under `instance/` and `logs/`.
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
## Application Architecture
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
The application follows a blueprint + service-layer architecture.
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
* Flask blueprints define routes and request handling.
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
* Business logic should live in `services.py` modules, not inside route handlers.
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
* Route handlers should remain thin and delegate logic to service functions.
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
* Reusable helpers belong in `utils.py` or dedicated service modules.
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
* Database operations should be encapsulated in services whenever possible.
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
When modifying behavior, prefer updating the relevant service layer rather than embedding logic directly in routes.
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
## Build, Test, and Development Commands
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
Create or activate the virtual environment and install dependencies:
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
```
.\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
pip install -r requirements-dev.txt
```
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
Run the app locally:
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
```
python app.py
```
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
Operational tasks can be executed through Flask CLI commands, for example:
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
```
flask --app app:create_app create-admin --email user@example.com
```
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
## Coding Style & Naming Conventions
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
Follow `.editorconfig`:
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
* UTF-8 encoding
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
* LF line endings
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
* final newline
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
* 4-space indentation for Python
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
Use lowercase module names with underscores. Match existing test naming patterns such as `test_support_routes.py`. Prefer small blueprint-local route handlers and move reusable logic into `services.py`, `utils.py`, or CLI helpers.
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
## Testing & Validation Rules
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
Pytest is configured in `.pytest.ini` to discover tests from `tests/`.
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
Every code change must include appropriate pytest coverage. Add tests for behavior changes, especially route protection, lifecycle transitions, and CLI flows. Prefer behavior-focused tests rather than implementation-detail tests.
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
Codex must use unique synthetic phone numbers for distinct test or validation accounts whenever phones are assigned; prefer existing factories and fixtures. Never use real phone numbers or send real SMS. Follow General Workflow Protocols Section 7.L for collision avoidance, isolated duplicate-phone rejection scenarios, persistent DEVELOPMENT authorization, and preservation of uniqueness enforcement.
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
Use the smallest validation sequence that fully proves the current work unit.
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
During implementation, run focused tests for the directly affected behavior and any additional targeted integration, migration, concurrency, lifecycle, or cross-surface tests materially required by the change.
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
At the final repository-wide validation gate, run the canonical full-suite coverage command:
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
```
pytest -q -n auto --dist=worksteal --cov=app --cov-report=term-missing --cov-fail-under=100
```
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
Because this command executes the complete repository test suite while also measuring coverage, a successful run satisfies both the full-suite pass requirement and the 100% repository coverage requirement.
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
Do not routinely run a separate:
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
```
pytest -q -n auto
```
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
immediately before or after the canonical coverage command merely to execute the same test population twice.
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
Run an additional no-coverage full suite only when it produces materially distinct evidence, such as diagnosing flakiness, concurrency or timing behavior, coverage-instrumentation effects, a different environment/database/worker configuration, or another specifically identified risk.
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
If focused or canonical validation fails, fix the code or tests and rerun the affected validation. Rerun broader validation when subsequent changes could invalidate its prior result.
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
Never leave the repository with failing tests or coverage below 100%.
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
## Commit & Pull Request Guidelines
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
Use short, imperative commit messages with a scoped feature label, for example:
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
```
Cargo 6C Phase 3: add cleared inflow webhook boundary
```
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
Format:
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
```
<initiative/area>: <specific change>
```
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
Keep commits focused and reviewable. Pull requests should explain the operational or user-visible impact, note schema or CLI changes, link the related issue if one exists, and include screenshots when modifying `templates/` or `static/`.
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
## Security & Configuration Tips
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
Secrets must be stored in `.env`, never committed to the repository. Treat CSRF protection, rate limiting, session timeout logic, and admin flows as high-risk areas. Preserve existing safeguards and validate them with tests before merging.
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
## Cargo Development Model
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
This repository uses a **Cargo-based incremental development model**.
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
* Each Cargo phase represents a small, isolated repository change.
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
* Modify the smallest possible set of files required to implement the phase.
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
* Inspect the current codebase before editing and briefly explain the plan.
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
* Preserve existing behavior unless the Cargo design explicitly requires modification.
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
* When logic changes, add or update pytest tests to maintain full coverage.
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
* Every Cargo phase must leave the repository fully passing with 100% test coverage.
